# Suite runner-process (plan 04-20, D-20, D-21, D-23, D-32): tests/runner-process-tap.sh runs the
# real ivs-run of each preset.
# runner-process.scenario.<id>: one case per line of data/expected/runner-scenarios.ids (exit
#   code, outcome, exit.code, outcome.detail, `ivsr 3` first and `report.end <n>` last);
#   needs-user-exec needs an operator EXEC the gate never has and is a visible skip; since plan 07-10
#   (D-31) so is owner-exec-title, the template of the owner-local route (docs/owner-images.md), and the
#   gate prints one `-- SKIPPED <preset>/<suite>/<id>: <reason>` line per skipped id and preset.
# runner-process.mutant.<ID>: one case per program mutation of data/expected/diag-mutations.ids,
#   guest-fail naming its clause (D-32), and (plan 05-11) one per row of
#   data/expected/diag-mutations-ctrl-psg.ids, judged by the outcome and exit code of the row
#   (guest-fail 10, timeout 11, pass 0).
# runner-process.repeat.<id>, runner-process.slices.<id> (--slice-ticks 1 and 7) and
#   runner-process.cwd (run from another directory): byte-equal reports apart from host.calls;
#   the slicing, repeat and directory variants cover diag-boot-pass, diag-input-pass and, since plan
#   05-11 (cost cap V-16), diag-ctrl-psg-pass (runner-process.cwd.diag-ctrl-psg-pass).
# Plan 07-07 (D-23, D-06, A-16): the four scenarios demo-lamp-tone-{pass,idle,shift,swap} are scenario cases;
#   runner-process.repeat/slices/cwd.demo-lamp-tone-pass repeat the pass scenario only (cost cap);
#   runner-process.pair.<idA>.<idB>: the metamorphic judge, one case per line of data/expected/scenario-pairs.ids (two reports of the
#   same run related by differ and equal keys, input.consumed equal to input.events, an idle run silent);
#   runner-process.judge.pair-video-equal, .input-not-consumed and .idle-audible: fake report pairs that the pair judge must reject.
# Plan 09-01 (D-09, D-09-01, A-03, A-04): demo-lamp-tone-shift and demo-lamp-tone-swap are column-5 `release-only`
#   rows: they run on the Release preset and are judged statically on the sanitized Debug preset by the derivation
#   equality of docs/video/demo-lamp-tone-1-derivation.md, with a one-value stand-in proving the judgement can fail.
#   The ids are the same in both presets, none is skip-ok and tests-skipped does not move; the pair with a
#   release-only member is judged by its inventory form on the sanitized preset and by check_pair on Release. The
#   diagnostic line before the trailer gains `variants=` and `variant-static=`, the counts of those rows in each mode.
# Plan 07-08 (D-25, A-14, A-17): runner-process.mutant.SCN-*, one case per row of data/expected/diag-mutations-scn.ids (12 mutants and one
#   negative row of the Lamp Tone program; stub-window rows patch boot-stub-2.exec; a fails column states the complete failing set).
# Plan 07-11 (D-31, T-07-29): runner-process.refuse-user.audio-raw, .psg-log-out and .audio-wav: each option is refused for a scenario with a
#   `content ... user` line (exit 14, one stderr line `<option> refused: content user`, no report, no file).
# Plan 14-03 (SCN-03, D-47, criterion 4): runner-process.cart-user.refuse-derived - a scratch scenario naming all three user roles
#   (`content exec user`, `content grom user`, `content cart user`) is refused for each of the six derived-output options (--frame-ppm,
#   --video-raw, --audio-raw, --psg-log-out, --audio-wav, --update-golden): exit 14, exactly one stderr line, no report and no file, with
#   the three audio options given together naming the first of their fixed order. The same case proves the snapshot rule to be the ABSENCE
#   of a seventh refusal: --roundtrip-at-ticks is accepted for that scenario, writes no file, leaves the report equal to the plain run apart
#   from host.calls, and must have made at least one cut.
# Plan 14-03 (SCN-03, D-47, criterion 3): runner-process.judge.flatwrap-roundtrip - a container the project's own wrap tool wrote loads as
#   `content cart user` with slot.cart.loaded 1, slot.cart.status ok, slot.cart.length_bytes equal to the file's size and slot.cart.sha256
#   equal to the digest the tool printed; the `exec:` label stays `-`. The equality is proved live: flipping one byte of the tool's input
#   moves both the printed digest and the report's value, so a judge that accepted the same digest twice cannot pass.
# Plan 14-03 (SCN-03, criterion 4, V10): runner-process.judge.owner-run-cart-pass - the helper's `--cart` option runs its pass path on
#   project-made zero-filled files and a container the tool wrote into refs/, printing the banner first `exec: -`, slot.exec.loaded 1,
#   slot.cart.loaded 1 and slot.cart.sha256 equal to the digest the tool printed, with the outcome, exit code and outcome.detail pinned to
#   what the run measured. A cartridge inside the work tree outside refs/ is refused like the other two images, and a one-byte EXEC mutation
#   after --record is refused at load (IVS_E_DIGEST_MISMATCH, exit 14), so the pass direction is proved able to fail.
# Plan 07-11 (D-31, T-07-26 to T-07-28): runner-process.owner-run.<case>, the nine cases of the owner-local helper tools/owner-run.sh (usage,
#   ci-env, github-actions-env, in-tree-path, wrong-size, missing-lock, bad-lock-line, no-images-skip, identity-mismatch), each on a copy of the
#   helper in a scratch root outside the work tree with project-made zero-filled files only; runner-process.judge.owner-run-refusal rejects a
#   fake helper that exits 0 where it must refuse.
# runner-process.judge.<slug>: judge self-controls, each passing only when the judge fails a fake
#   runner for its intended reason (exit code, missing report.end, outcome and exit.code
#   disagreeing, a changed report body; plan 04-24: a removed demonstrating scenario).
# runner-process.outcomes-consistent: --help, docs/runner.md and data/expected/runner-outcomes.ids
#   agree; runner-process.outcomes-covered: every outcome has a running scenario.
# runner-process.watchdog: the watchdog self-control (stopped at once it stays silent; it stops a
#   run past its limit).
# Plan 15-01 (TOOL-08, D-48, D-35): the host shim. The suite's seventh argument is the built build-only shared object
#   libivs_retro and the eighth is the fake frontend ivs_host_harness; both are `$<TARGET_FILE:...>` paths, never an
#   environment variable. runner-process.host.digests runs the frontend once on the project's own lamp-tone.ivsc (boot
#   stub 2, a made zero GROM) and requires a positive frame count before anything is compared, then the nine `digest.`
#   lines it printed to equal data/golden/demo-lamp-tone-pass.digests byte for byte under exactly the two comparisons
#   check_one_golden makes: the sorted line set and the observed domain set. A missing argument is `not ok` with that
#   reason, never a skip, so a configuration without the object cannot pass.
#   runner-process.host.exports runs tools/check/host-symbols.sh over the same object: the exported set equals
#   data/expected/host-symbols.ids in BOTH directions (a listed name the object does not export, an exported name the
#   list does not carry), no exported name carries a palette word, and every undefined name is in
#   core-symbols-allow.txt or data/expected/host-imports.ids once the three sanitizer prefixes are subtracted. The
#   missing-name direction is proved live inside the case: a scratch ids copy with one name appended must be refused
#   with `-- FAIL host-symbols-missing: ivs_not_defined`.
# Plan 15-02 (TOOL-08, D-48): the shaped-entry contract, judged on the SAME pass-mode run host.digests makes (no second
#   harness process). runner-process.host.api: the shape version the object answers, a host with a NULL input_poll
#   REFUSED rather than ignored beside a complete host accepted, and the av_info metadata a frontend places a frame
#   with - 159 x 96 base and max, frame_phase_ticks 0, nominal_frame_ticks 59736, the fps pair 3579545/59736 and
#   sample_rate 48000, each pinned in the harness from data/profiles/intv-2609-ntsc-family-p1.json's own fields and
#   from the converter the shim uses. runner-process.host.frames: frames 13 required first (so a run that delivered
#   nothing cannot pass on a comparison of nothing), then video.plane.bytes 198432 = 13 x 159 x 96 index-plane bytes
#   (not the golden's 198848 slot bytes, which include the core's 32-byte header), frame.geometry ok 13 and
#   pixel.probes ok 13 equal to the frame count, a positive audio.frames with audio.bytes == 2 x audio.frames (mono
#   signed 16-bit), and the poll's six `<at_tick> <c0> <c1>` words equal to the scenario's - derived at run time from
#   data/scenarios/demo-lamp-tone-pass.ivss and data/expected/input-bits.ids, never typed in the case.
#   runner-process.host.unload: `unload.digest.refused 1`, and a `digest.` line BEFORE it by line number, so the
#   order proves the digests were taken while the machine was still loaded - a harness that only pretended to unload
#   cannot pass.
#   runner-process.host.empty-poll: the same harness and the same drive with NO input, judged in three limbs each able
#   to fail on its own - at least one digest line differs from the pass golden (so a judge that compared an output
#   with itself fails), digest.images is EQUAL to the pass golden (so a shim that stopped rendering fails, and a
#   difference stays attributable to the input alone), and every non-input digest line the IDLE golden carries
#   (eight today; its `# domains:` line reads input=none) equals that golden's, with digest.input differing from the
#   pass golden's.
# Plan 15-04 (TOOL-08, review CR-01, WR-01 and WR-02): the two measured defects of the shim and the guard on the
#   guard, each made observable through the frontend's own answers. runner-process.judge.host-schedule: the input schedule is bounded and REFUSES rather than
#   truncates, in two limbs - `--schedule-full` (5000 events pending in one drain) requires `schedule.runs.ok 0`,
#   `schedule.frames 0` and a `schedule.polled` above zero and below 5000, so the refusal came from the schedule's
#   bound while the host still had events left; `--schedule-long` (8000 events over two calls, 4000 at a time)
#   requires both calls to succeed and all 8000 to be polled, so the bound is on OUTSTANDING events and a long run
#   cannot lose input. The pair is the non-vacuity: one count fits over a lifetime, one does not fit at once.
#   runner-process.judge.host-load-refused: a refused load leaves nothing committed - the instance digest is unmoved by a
#   refused four-byte-cartridge load, the run is refused, and the same call with the right images then succeeds and
#   moves the digest, so the refusal is not a dead machine and a retry is meaningful.
#   runner-process.judge.host-two-limb: the two limbs that make the judge self-control's own guard able to fail. A
#   self_control now requires the check's OBSERVED token set to equal the DECLARED one, so (a) the pass golden's lines
#   with `frames 13`, which an empty-poll judge finds identical to the pass output (empty-poll-identical) AND still
#   carrying the pass run's input (empty-poll-idle), must be refused as judge-wrong-reason rather than accepted because
#   the declared token is present; and (b) a declaration claiming a limb the check never exercised (frames-zero AND
#   digest-diff on the frames-zero stand-in, whose check returns before the digests) must be refused too.
#   runner-process.judge.host-vacuous and runner-process.judge.host-digest-diff: the phase's two COMMITTED stand-ins
#   (criterion 3.3), not scratch probes. Each is a fake frontend this suite writes into its temp dir and judges with
#   the SAME check_host the real run is judged by: host-vacuous prints the pass golden's nine lines with `frames 0`
#   and must be refused for frames-zero; host-digest-diff prints them with `digest.input` replaced by the empty-poll
#   run's OBSERVED value and `frames 13` and must be refused for digest-diff. Each differs from a passing output in
#   one limb only, so a rejection for another token is judge-wrong-reason and an acceptance is judge-vacuous.
